WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.335
Django templates support autoescaping
concepts that reduce common output risks when

2
00:00:09.415 --> 00:00:11.109
used correctly.

3
00:00:11.189 --> 00:00:16.998
Developers still need to know where data
is being placed.

4
00:00:17.078 --> 00:00:22.040
HTML text, attributes, URLs, JavaScript,
JSON,

5
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:26.114
and CSS are different output contexts.

6
00:00:26.194 --> 00:00:34.302
User-controlled content that is safe as
plain text may be unsafe if placed into a

7
00:00:34.382 --> 00:00:38.255
script block, an event handler, a URL,

8
00:00:38.335 --> 00:00:44.507
or a client-side rendering path without
the right handling.

9
00:00:44.587 --> 00:00:51.727
Template safety is context-aware, not
automatic in every situation.

10
00:00:51.807 --> 00:01:00.400
Cross-site scripting at a high level
occurs when untrusted data becomes executable

11
00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.684
or behavior-changing content in a user's
browser.

12
00:01:05.764 --> 00:01:11.452
The defensive pattern is to validate what
can be stored,

13
00:01:11.532 --> 00:01:16.494
render with the appropriate escaping
behavior,

14
00:01:16.574 --> 00:01:21.172
avoid unnecessary escape-bypass features,

15
00:01:21.252 --> 00:01:29.361
and handle rich user-generated content
through constrained, reviewed paths.

16
00:01:29.441 --> 00:01:33.798
Security headers can add defense in depth,

17
00:01:33.878 --> 00:01:42.107
but they do not replace correct rendering
decisions in templates and responses.

18
00:01:42.187 --> 00:01:46.907
Safe redirects are another response
concern.

19
00:01:46.987 --> 00:01:54.853
Redirect targets should be constrained so
users are not sent to unintended or

20
00:01:54.933 --> 00:02:00.137
untrusted destinations based on unchecked
input.

21
00:02:00.217 --> 00:02:08.689
Redirect logic often appears in login
flows, invitation flows, account recovery,

22
00:02:08.769 --> 00:02:16.756
and post-action navigation, so it is easy
to treat it as harmless convenience.

23
00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:25.792
A secure design treats redirects as
security-sensitive output because they influence

24
00:02:25.872 --> 00:02:29.866
where users and tokens may travel next.

25
00:02:29.946 --> 00:02:36.118
Response data minimization and error
behavior also matter.

26
00:02:36.198 --> 00:02:42.733
Views should return the fields needed for
the caller and no more.

27
00:02:42.813 --> 00:02:51.526
Error pages should avoid internal details
while still giving operators the logs they

28
00:02:51.606 --> 00:02:52.212
need.

29
00:02:52.292 --> 00:03:01.005
API responses should not expose hidden
model fields simply because serialization is

30
00:03:01.085 --> 00:03:02.416
convenient.

31
00:03:02.496 --> 00:03:09.758
Whether the response is HTML, JSON, a
redirect, a download, or an error,

32
00:03:09.838 --> 00:03:17.220
the application should intentionally
decide what the caller receives.
